  "query_preview": "..\n\nToday through Saturday...\n\nHighs into next week will remain generally in the 40s and 50s.\n \nLate this afternoon into Monday, an upper low will clip southeast \nMontana, bringing a 30-80% chance (highest near the Dakota border)\nof precipitation east of Billings. Along the Dakota border, \nthere is a 40% chance of more than 0.25\". Over the rest of the \neast, there is generally a less than 50% chance for more than \n0.10\". As temperatures drop overnight, a little wet snow may \noccur, although little to no accumulation is expected. Outside of \nthe higher hills, there is a less than 20% chance for more than a \ntrace of snow. During this time, easterly low level flow increases\nover the plains and may result in areas of fog and low clouds \nacross the western foothills and nearby plains tonight into Monday\nmorning.\n\nA more impactful system approaches from the west Monday afternoon\ninto Wednesday as an upper trough aggressively pushes into the \nnorthern Rockies. The exact track of the associated jet streak \nwill decide where the best upper dynamics hit, but at this time \na decent widespread precipitation event is expected (40-70% \nchance over the plains, 60-90% over the mountains). Currently,\nthere is a 20-40% chance for at least 0.25\" of precip over much of\nthe plains. Over the mountains, snow is favored over SW-NW \naspects of the Beartooth/Absaroka and Crazy mountains, with a \n40-60% chance for several inches of snow and locally higher\namounts over the highest peaks. Over the northern Bighorn \nmountains, generally 1 to 2\" of snow are expected (70% chance) \nabove 7000 ft, with locally higher amounts over the tallest peaks.\n\nFor the end of the work week into the weekend, the pattern is\nexpected to remain unsettled under southwest flow, allowing for \noccasional rounds of precipitation to continue. Archer",
